            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for the issue!\r\n\r\n'mini.splitjoin' is customizable for what it considers to be an argument and what action it will perform before/after split/join. Here is [documentation of available `config` settings](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/445f73cff53987432cb9eea41c4dc25e4a4c3c04/doc/mini-splitjoin.txt#L107).\r\n\r\nThis can also be [customized per filetype](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/445f73cff53987432cb9eea41c4dc25e4a4c3c04/doc/mini-splitjoin.txt#L58-L60). In your case I suggest creating file 'after/ftplugin/nix.lua' in your config directory which creates proper `vim.b.minisplitjoin_config`.\r\n\r\nAfter some trial and error, here is something that seems to work good enough:\r\n\r\n```lua\r\nif _G.MiniSplitjoin ~= nil then\r\n  -- Pad square brackets with single space after join\r\n  local pad_brackets = MiniSplitjoin.gen_hook.pad_brackets({ brackets = { '%b[]' } })\r\n\r\n  vim.b.minisplitjoin_config = {\r\n    detect = { separator = '[^[]%f[ ] +' },\r\n    join = { hooks_post = { pad_brackets } },\r\n  }\r\nend\r\n```\r\n\r\nIt would have been shorter if there were no padding inside `[]` because 'mini.splitjoin' treats first space as separator too.\r\nIf you need it to work not only with square brackets, then some hackery here might be needed.\r\n\r\nClosing as not a bug.",
